WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.271
Hi, I'm Orchid!

00:00:02.271 --> 00:00:06.521
Agents now split three ways: code, a decision model, and a reasoning model.

00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:08.156
Here's how the split works.

00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:12.652
The tiers differ by output type, not difficulty.

00:00:12.652 --> 00:00:16.605
And the decision tier can't see across its options, so code builds

00:00:16.605 --> 00:00:17.923
every relation for it.

00:00:19.525 --> 00:00:23.413
An exact rule, like stopping after ten actions, belongs in code.

00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:27.150
If it picks, scores, or answers yes or no, it goes to

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.255
the decision model, Jev.

00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:31.477
If it creates text, it stays with the reasoning model.

00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:36.000
Stanley, a coding agent built this way, shows it.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.656
Code computes facts first, and gates which workflows may be offered.

00:00:40.076 --> 00:00:43.398
Jev gets one question: pick a workflow, or cannot tell.

00:00:43.398 --> 00:00:45.724
It returns a probability for each option.

00:00:46.144 --> 00:00:50.144
Then fixed thresholds decide, including a margin: the top pick must beat

00:00:50.144 --> 00:00:52.144
the runner-up by point one five.

00:00:52.564 --> 00:00:55.141
Fail a test and the outcome is cannot tell.

00:00:55.141 --> 00:00:58.864
The model's answer is only an input to a decision that code makes.

00:01:00.708 --> 00:01:05.091
A robot arm's move is a joint choice across X, Y, Z

00:01:05.091 --> 00:01:06.917
and the gripper: eighty-one combinations.

00:01:06.917 --> 00:01:09.474
The xArm7 harness never asks for one.

00:01:09.893 --> 00:01:13.723
Instead it asks four separate questions, one sign per axis.

00:01:13.723 --> 00:01:17.935
The model chooses direction; code chooses distance, and checks the workspace.

00:01:19.425 --> 00:01:23.473
The headline says up to a hundred and ninety-three point six times faster.

00:01:23.473 --> 00:01:26.588
Measured per call in that trial: four point five seven.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.921
Making a decision cheap pushes the hard work back into deterministic code,

00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:33.409
where you can read it.

00:01:36.192 --> 00:01:41.113
To recap: sort by output type, let code build relations, and let thresholds decide.

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:43.645
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:44.248
I'm Orchid.

00:01:44.248 --> 00:01:44.550
Bye!
